{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pulls/11","id":5024959756,"node_id":"PR_kwDOVELxLc8AAAABK4LNDA","html_url":"https://github.com/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pull/11","diff_url":"https://github.com/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pull/11.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pull/11.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/issues/11","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pulls/11/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pulls/11/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/issues/11/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/adeotek/coding-agent-bench/statuses/c01de0425381d32c1a29bf942915051edc28a964","number":11,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"[feat:bench] Task audit over all 89: 78 healthy, 8 broken, 3 host-limited — and a run list that enforces it","user":{"login":"adeotek","id":6872816,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4NzI4MTY=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6872816?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek","html_url":"https://github.com/adeotek","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adeotek/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The gate before an 89-task run: **is every cell a task at all?** Harbor ships the two agents that answer it, and neither costs a token: `oracle` runs the reference solution (must pass), `nop` does nothing (must fail).\n\n## Result — all 89 cells audited (15 jobs, 2026-10-26/27)\n\n| verdict | cells | meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| **healthy** | **78** | oracle passes, nop fails — a real task |\n| **task-side defect** | **8** | the task or its reference solution is broken |\n| **host-limited** | **3** | unpassable *on this machine*, for environmental reasons |\n| vacuous / both-broken / no-verdict | 0 | no verifier here is satisfiable by inaction |\n\n### The 8 defects (each re-verified at 2 attempts, `cab-audit-20261027-014246-145939`)\n\n| cell | cause |\n|---|---|\n| `build-cython-ext` | verifier **clones pyknotid from GitHub** at verification time and asserts the upstream suite; oracle 0/3 — after all four agents passed the same cell **8/8** a day earlier |\n| `build-pmars` | solution installs `dpkg-dev=1.22.21` — no longer in the repos |\n| `make-doom-for-mips` | solution calls `clang`; not in the image |\n| `mcmc-sampling-stan` | `rstan` never installs |\n| `rstan-to-pystan` | no `/app/alpha_est.csv` produced |\n| `protein-assembly` | no `/app/gblock.txt` produced |\n| `tune-mjcf` | no artifacts produced |\n| `train-fasttext` | asserts `accuracy > 0.62`; the reference measures **0.619725** — oracle 0/1 then **1.0, 1.0**: passes or fails on training noise |\n\n### The 3 host-limited — *not* task defects\n\nThis host is a **QEMU guest with a generic vCPU and no `/dev/kvm`**: `mteb-leaderboard` dies with `Illegal instruction (core dumped)`, and both `qemu-*` cells need hardware virtualization. Its twin `mteb-retrieve` is healthy here, so the failure is per-image. They get their own registry (`docs/host-limits.json`) and their own column, because a host's limits must never be recorded as claims about a task.\n\n## What may be measured — the audit's payload\n\nFinding the broken cells is only half the job; the other half is not paying for them. The run\nlists are **derived** from the audit job store, never hand-written:\n\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `docs/tb2-measurable.txt` | **78** cells whose own oracle passes *here* — the list a measured run should use |\n| `docs/tb2-watch.txt` | the 11 that are not healthy, each with its class (`broken-task`, `host-limited`) |\n\n`render_config.py` **drops** watch-listed cells from every non-audit profile, by name, on stderr —\na cell whose reference solution fails has no agent signal in either direction, so scoring one is\nhow a fabricated regression gets published. `ALLOW_DEFECTIVE=1` is the manual override; the\n`audit` profile is exempt because re-finding them is its job. `make audit-watch` re-audits the\nbroken cells (zero tokens) and refreshes both lists, so a cell repaired upstream walks back in by\nits own evidence.\n\nVisible effect today: the frozen 24-cell subset renders **23 tasks (92 trials instead of 96)**. No\nscore moves — `build-cython-ext` was already excluded from scoring by `docs/task-side.json` — so\nthe rounds stay comparable while the tokens stop.\n\n## Consequence\n\nThe ceiling of a full-89 run **on this machine** is 86 cells, and 8 of those cannot produce an agent signal — the honest comparison set is **78**. And `build-cython-ext` rotted from \"all agents pass\" to \"oracle fails\" **inside one day**, so the audit is a gate to re-run before every round.\n\n## What's in the PR\n\n| piece | what it does |\n|---|---|\n| `render_config.py` audit profile | `oracle` + `nop`, no model, no key; `TASK_FILE=` drives it |\n| `scripts/audit.sh` | batches the list, **prunes each batch's images**, disk guard per batch, resumable (re-splits only what is still pending) |\n| `docs/tb2-all.txt` | the 89 tracked cells |\n| `docs/host-limits.json` | cells this host cannot measure, with the reason and the job id |\n| `bench_report.py audit` | healthy / **host-limited** / broken-task / vacuous / both-broken / no-verdict / unknown, `--json`, `--out=`, `--host-limits=` |\n| `docs/task-defects.md` | the full evidence table and what it changes |\n| `scripts/runlist.sh` + `docs/tb2-{measurable,watch}.txt` | derive the run list from the audit (78 measurable, 11 watched) |\n| `scripts/audit-watch.sh` + `make audit-watch` | re-audit the unhealthy cells, zero tokens; fixed cells return automatically |\n| render-time guard | non-audit profiles drop non-measurable cells, by name; `ALLOW_DEFECTIVE=1` overrides |\n| both living reports | the audit section, in the same commit |\n\n**Why batching:** images ranged 0.03–21.5 GB; a 0.65 GB mean over 89 cells hid `mteb-retrieve` at 21.5 GB, which filled the disk and killed a sweep with `ENOSPC`. Batches now run from smallest image up with a guard that prunes and refuses below a floor.\n\n## Tests\n\n`test/bench_report_audit_test.py` — 21 checks: every classifier branch, `nop` missing → **unknown** (an incomplete audit cannot look clean), no-verdict never folded into a side, **host-limited relabelled and never counted as a defect**, the reason travels with the cell, a missing registry is empty and an invalid one fails loudly.\n\nNew `test/bench_runlist_guard_test.py` pins the derivation: measurable excludes broken **and** host-limited, the watch list is the exact complement with classes, the guard drops-and-says-so, the audit profile is exempt, `ALLOW_DEFECTIVE=1` overrides, an all-dropped run is refused rather than launched empty, and an absent registry is *not* an allow-list.\n\n`make test`: **102 checks**, exit 0.","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:31:31Z","updated_at":"2026-10-27T08:38:42Z","closed_at":"2026-10-27T08:38:38Z","merged_at":"2026-10-27T08:38:38Z","merge_commit_sha":"0b14eb0648ed52fc2ddab898cbebfdcec0c71b82","assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"head":{"label":"adeotek:feat/task-audit"